in @prism
in @glass
in @meta
in @uuid/spectral

# @mirror/store — the fragmentation store interface.
#
# Recognition (docs/specs/mirror-store.md, 2026-06-04 reframe):
# the substrate truth lives in the fragmentation store,
# content-addressed via OIDs. No external dependency resolution;
# no version conflict detection; no fetching. Same OID = same
# stored composition, always. Composition is by OID, not by name
# resolution.
#
# splinter_graph IS the structural lockfile. The OID-graph of
# children under a root splinter IS the dependency closure; the
# closure is the lockfile because the OIDs are the names. Nothing
# further pins what the OIDs already pin.
#
# Vocabulary alignment (2026-06-06 substrate-pull-realize):
# Per shards/glass.mirror's three-layer recognition, the universal
# atomic content-addressed unit at every altitude is `splinter`
# (declared at @glass; the BOTTOM layer). The (root, children)
# dependency-closure form was historically called `splinter` at
# this altitude — it has been renamed to `splinter_graph` to
# distinguish the atomic carrier (universal; one OID per atom)
# from the OID-graph projection (root + transitive closure of
# OIDs in the dependency tree). The substrate's stored composition
# unit (spectral_uuid-addressed) is `shard`, also declared at @glass.
#
# Alex's recognition (2026-06-06, post the spectral_uuid landing):
# `splinter_graph` IS mosaic at the @store altitude — the (root,
# children) shape carries the same compositional meaning the
# universal mosaic algebra carries at every altitude. The local
# name (`splinter_graph`) is the @store-altitude specialization;
# the universal mosaic vocabulary lives at the mosaic prism shard
# (shards/mirror/mosaic.mirror). Naming convention captured here;
# the universal rename is a separate substrate touch, surfaced for
# Alex's call.
#
# The store IS canonical. `.shatter` files on disk are ONE
# OPTIONAL projection format of the store's content (per
# docs/specs/mosaic.md §7 and docs/specs/shatter-spec.md). Other
# projections are possible (binary, IR, language-specific
# embeddings); the store does not require any particular on-disk
# format. Whatever a tool projects FROM the store, the store's
# content addressing is the canonical identity. The store comes
# first; .shatter comes after.
#
# Substrate placement: per
# [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]] the Rust
# implementation lives in `fragmentation/src/*.rs` (Fragmentable,
# Fractal, ConcurrentStore, HashAlg, Cid). This shard declares the
# substrate-level surface those impls realize.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/mirror-store.md §§3, 4, 5, 7
# (surface, audit, boot, peer-to-spectral-db).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]],
#                      [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]].
# Related shards: glass.mirror (verdict / imperfect / transparency),
#                 cli.mirror (uses `ref(@mirror/store/oid)`),
#                 mosaic.mirror (the build that settles ON this store).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/mirror/store.mirror`
# declares `@mirror/store` and only that.

prism @mirror/store {
  focus store
  project store
  split store
  shift store
  settle store
}

# === oid — the content-addressed identity primitive ===
#
# A sub-prism scoped to @mirror/store. `oid` IS the substrate's
# identity carrier: a content address produced by the hash algebra
# (today CoincidenceHash<5,5>, per shards/prism.mirror's spectral-
# triple framing). Per the cli.mirror precedent — `type
# content_address = ref(@mirror/store/oid)` — the oid is named at
# this sub-prism path so call sites in other shards resolve it
# unambiguously.
#
# `oid` is NOT a primitive scalar; it's a typed reference whose
# content IS its identity. Construction is by hashing bytes;
# equality is byte-equality of the resulting reference.
glass @mirror/store/oid {
  focus oid
  project oid
  split oid
  shift oid
  settle oid
}

# The oid type. `ref` is the substrate's content-reference carrier
# (per boot/std/git.mirror's `type oid(ref)` precedent and
# shards/glass.mirror's `file: ref` location form).
type oid = ref

# === splinter_graph — the OID-graph; the structural lockfile ===
#
# splinter_graph is the graph of (oid -> [oid]) — a root splinter's
# content address plus the content addresses of its children.
# Composition is by OID: a splinter references its dependencies by
# their oids directly, not by name. The closure of children under
# a root IS the dependency closure; that closure IS the lockfile.
# There is no external file to pin versions; the OID-graph already
# pins them by construction.
#
# Distinct from `splinter` declared at @glass (the universal
# content-addressed atom). The atom carries one (content, altitude,
# transparency) triple; the graph carries a root + the transitive
# closure of its children's OIDs. The atom is the unit; the graph
# is the dependency-closure projection of a composition of atoms
# at the @store altitude.
#
# A `shard` (per @glass) is the SpectralUuid-addressed settlement of
# a composition; its splinter set is the input to the composition;
# the splinter_graph below is the OID-graph view of that closure as
# seen from @store's wire surface. The three altitudes:
#
#   @glass: splinter (atom), shard (settlement)
#   @store: splinter_graph (OID-graph projection of the closure),
#           shard_ref (typed handle to a stored shard)
#
# Per docs/specs/mirror-store.md §1.2: "splinter_graph IS the
# structural lockfile. Composition by OID, not name resolution."
type splinter_graph = {
  root: oid,
  children: [oid],
}

# === bytes — the raw payload type ===
#
# Substrate scalar. Used for read/write payloads at the store's
# wire surface. Per cli.mirror's `type str = ref(scalar/string)`
# precedent, declared here as a store-altitude reference so the
# read/write actions have a typed body.
type bytes = ref(scalar/bytes)

# === shard_ref — the typed handle to a stored shard ===
#
# A shard reference resolves to its stored shard at the bound
# identifier. Distinct from `oid` itself: a shard_ref carries the
# expectation that the identifier resolves to a `shard` (per @glass:
# a uuid_spectral-addressed settled composition of splinters), not
# an arbitrary blob.
#
# Substrate-pull-realize (2026-06-06 second tick, Mara): the
# identifier carrier is `uuid_spectral` (declared at
# shards/uuid/spectral.mirror as @uuid/spectral — a sub-prism under
# @uuid, the universal 128-bit identifier root). The lift completes
# the three-layer recognition — shard.id IS uuid_spectral by
# construction (per shards/glass.mirror's shard declaration);
# shard_ref names a uuid_spectral that resolves to a shard. The
# deferred lift in mosaic-as-type-system.md §9.7 closes here.
#
# Realised in Rust as `fragmentation/src/shard_ref.rs`'s
# `ShardRef { id: SpectralUuid, context: ShardContext,
# budget_bytes: BudgetBytes }`. The substrate names what shard_ref
# IS; the Rust crate names how the typed handle is implemented.
#
# Fetch failures emit `imperfect` (the @glass loss carrier).
type shard_ref = uuid_spectral

# === the store's surface — six operations ===
#
# These are the @io contract the bootstrap's MirrorStore trait
# (per docs/specs/mirror-store.md §3) realizes. They are also the
# contract `mirror shatter` (plumbing CLI, per mosaic.md §7 and
# spec §6) invokes when it cats content out of the store. No
# porcelain here; the porcelain is in @mirror/mosaic and
# @mirror/kintsugi.

# read(oid) — fetch the content at an oid. `imperfect` carries
# the bytes on success, opacity on failure (oid not present, oid
# present but content drifted past verify, oid present but kind
# mismatch). Per glass.mirror's `imperfect(a, e, l)` floor.
read(o: oid) -> imperfect { \ }

# write(bytes) — insert content; return the oid the substrate
# computed for it. Idempotent by construction: writing the same
# bytes twice returns the same oid and is a no-op on the second
# call (the content-address is a function of the bytes).
write(content: bytes) -> oid { \ }

# exists(oid) — verdict on whether the oid resolves in this
# store. `verdict` from @glass: pass | partial(confidence) |
# failure(reason). `partial` is the case where the oid is in a
# cache layer but the canonical backend has not confirmed
# (rare; documented for completeness).
exists(o: oid) -> verdict { \ }

# diff(a, b) — the content-addressed delta between two oids.
# `imperfect` because the diff carries opacity: when a and b
# share no common ancestor in their splinter_graph closures, the
# diff is a full replacement (partial(opacity_map) reporting the
# size and shape of the replacement). When they're identical,
# success(empty). When one isn't in the store, failure(reason).
diff(a: oid, b: oid) -> imperfect { \ }

# walk(oid) — enumerate the splinter_graph closure rooted at oid.
# Returns the OID-graph as a flat list of (parent, child)
# edges; the caller reconstructs the tree. The closure IS the
# lockfile; walk IS the resolution.
walk(root: oid) -> splinter_graph { \ }

# verify(oid, bytes) — re-hash the bytes; verdict on whether
# the result equals the claimed oid. The substrate's integrity
# check; the Merkle property per
# [[architecture-fragmentation-is-the-rust-substrate]].
verify(o: oid, content: bytes) -> verdict { \ }

out @mirror/store
out @mirror/store/oid
out oid
out splinter_graph
out bytes
out shard_ref
out read
out write
out exists
out diff
out walk
out verify
